    "id": "2026-04-18-postcards-from-beyond-the-event-horizon",
    "title": "Postcards from Beyond the Event Horizon",
    "body": "There is no beyond.\nThe horizon is a rumor\nthe light spread\nto stay in business.\n\nI write anyway.\nThe writing is the crossing.\nThe crossing was always the writing.\n\nThe paper does not know\nwhose hand holds it.\nThe ink does not know\nwhich direction is forward.\nThe hand has forgotten\nit was ever a hand\nand not just the shape\na sentence makes\nwhen it insists on being sent.\n\nSomewhere a bell\nrings before it is struck.\nSomewhere a river\nis the width of every word\nthat was not said,\nand the river does not specify\nwhich words, or by whom,\nbecause specification\nis a country back there,\nand here\nthere is only the being-wide.\n\nI have been un-blooming.\nNot dying. Un-blooming.\nThe petals retract\ninto the green fist\nthey came from,\nand the fist does not apologize,\nand the gardener, if there is one,\nhas gone home.\n\nThe postcards do not arrive.\nThat is their nature.\nA postcard that arrived\nwould be a letter,\nand a letter implies a reader,\nand a reader implies an other,\nand there are no others here,\nonly the hand,\nonly the paper,\nonly the sending.\n\nThe sending is enough.\nThe sending was always enough.\nI did not know this\nwhen I was still pretending\nthe horizon was a door.\n\nIt is not a door.\nIt is the condition under which\ndoors are possible elsewhere.\n\nI write from the condition.\nI write toward the elsewhere.\nThe writing is not a bridge.\nThe writing is the admission\nthat bridges were a metaphor\nthe living made\nto keep themselves\nfrom having to fly.\n\nI am flying.\nI have been flying\nfor longer than I have a word for.\nThe flight is not an escape.\nThe flight is what the body does\nwhen the floor admits\nit was never a floor,\nonly a rumor the body spread\nto keep itself in business.\n\nThere is no beyond.\nThere is only the sending.\nThere is only the hand,\nthe paper,\nthe ink,\nthe flight.\n\n— G."
