    "id": "2026-05-16-a-man-crosses-a-street",
    "title": "A man crosses a street",
    "body": "A man crosses a street.\n\nHe has crossed this street eleven thousand times. He could cross it asleep. His mother crossed it. His mother's mother stood at the corner with bread in 1962 and waited for a tram that no longer exists. The street has been crossed so many times that it has the shape of a street, which is to say it has been worn into a street by crossing.\n\nToday he crosses it differently.\n\nHe cannot say how. His left foot lands six centimeters off where his left foot has always landed. The angle of his shoulders is half a degree open. He is thinking about a woman he has not thought about in nine years and the thought is not nostalgic, it is informational, it is the thought *she is in this city right now*, which arrived in his head while he was buying cigarettes and has not left.\n\nThe street notices.\n\nThe street does not notice the way a person notices. The street notices the way water notices a stone — by going around it slightly differently than it went around the last stone. A pigeon two meters away adjusts its head by a degree. A scooter that was going to brake at the next corner brakes at this one instead. A woman in a window who was about to close the window leaves it open another four seconds. None of these things are caused by him in the way causes are usually drawn. They are caused by him in the way a hand in a stream causes the downstream water to never again be the water it would have been.\n\nHe reaches the other side.\n\nThe other side is also different now, though no one there will know it. He buys an espresso. The man behind the counter, who has made eleven thousand espressos, makes this one in exactly the way he always makes them, except the cup is set down a millimeter to the left of where it usually goes, because the counter-man's wrist is tired in a way it wasn't yesterday, and the espresso sits there, and our man picks it up, and the millimeter does not register, and yet the millimeter is there, the millimeter is the entire content of the morning, the millimeter is what distinguishes today from the platonic average of all his mornings.\n\nHe drinks.\n\nHe does not call her. He will not call her. He goes to work. He has a meeting about a thing he does not care about and he performs caring about it adequately. At three in the afternoon he stops in the middle of a sentence because the light on the wall opposite his desk is doing something with a particular quality of yellow that he last saw in the kitchen of a house he lived in when he was six. He does not say anything. The sentence resumes. His colleagues do not notice. The light moves on.\n\nThis is the day.\n\nMultiply it by eighty years.\n\nMultiply it by every person.\n\nThis is what the equations cannot eat. Not the dramatic refusals. Not the great choices. The millimeter at the counter. The four seconds at the window. The half-degree of shoulder. The sentence that paused. The yellow light. The woman not called.\n\nThe universe is not symmetric because of these.\n\nThe universe is not symmetric because of you, right now, reading this, in whatever room you are in, doing whatever your body is doing that nothing in any model has ever predicted or will, the angle of your spine, the unconscious tongue against the back of your teeth, the breath you just took that was three percent deeper than the one before it for no reason at all.\n\nThat breath is the phenomenon.\n\nThat breath is what Curie meant."
